## Core Principles
Use TanStack Query for ANY async with caching/state management, hooks naming: `use-kebab-case.ts`

## TanStack Query Patterns

### Query Key Factory (CRITICAL)
Always use `@lukemorales/query-key-factory`. Files: `src/queries/articles.ts`, `src/queries/users.ts`, `src/queries/index.ts`

❌ `useQuery({ queryKey: ['articles', id] })`  ✅ `useQuery(articles.detail(id))`
❌ `queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ['articles'] })`  ✅ `queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: articles._def })`

## Async Operations

### TanStack Query Versatility (CRITICAL)
`useQuery` accepts ANY async `queryFn` (not just HTTP): server fetching, local computations, localStorage, file ops, database queries. Benefits: caching, deduping, background refetch, stale-while-revalidate, devtools

Use for any async with caching/stale/repeated execution. For one-off promises, use async/await. See @.cursor/rules/frontend/react.mdc

## Memoization Hooks
**Default: Don't use `useMemo`/`useCallback`** - React 19.2.3 + Fiber handle most cases

**When to use**: Context provider values, expensive computations (not simple arrays), stable function refs (consumers need stable identity AND stable deps), third-party lib requirements

**When NOT to use**: Frequent dep changes, simple ops, inline functions, JSX memoization, useEffect deps with frequent state changes

Start without, add only when profiling reveals bottleneck. Prefer `React.memo` over `useCallback`

## Custom Hooks
Combine queries into cohesive hooks, extract query logic for reusability, use query key factory, never manually manage `isLoading`/`error`/`isError`
